# Tier 1 — CPU, Registers and Processor State

**Extremely volatile**

This information can change in microseconds and is normally available only through specialized acquisition, debugging, hypervisor, crash-dump, or hardware mechanisms.

* [ ] CPU registers
* [ ] Instruction pointer / program counter
* [ ] Stack pointer
* [ ] CPU execution state
* [ ] CPU cache contents
* [ ] Processor-specific state
* [ ] Debug registers
* [ ] Hardware breakpoint state
* [ ] CPU virtualization state where obtainable

### Practical note

On an ordinary incident response engagement you normally **do not acquire CPU registers or CPU caches directly**.

They remain important conceptually because RFC 3227 places them at the top of the volatility hierarchy.

